.TH PASSWD 1 
.SH NAME
passwd \- change login password
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B passwd
[ name ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
This command changes (or installs) a password
associated with the user
.IR name
(your own name by default).
.PP
The program prompts for the old password and then for the new one.
The caller must supply both.
The new password must be typed twice, to forestall mistakes.
.PP
.B Note:
the old password is not asked for when the account has none \-
which is how every account on this system but
.B root
ships \-
nor when the caller is the super-user.
.PP
.B Note:
with no argument the name comes from
.IR getlogin (3),
which reads the /etc/utmp record for
.I this terminal
rather than mapping the user id.
A caller who has used
.IR su (1)
is therefore asked about the account they logged in as.
.PP
New passwords must be at least four characters long if they use
a sufficiently rich alphabet and at least six characters long
if monocase.
These rules are relaxed if you are insistent enough.
.PP
Only the owner of the name or the super-user may change a password;
the owner must prove he knows the old password.
.I Passwd
is set-user-id to root, which is how it comes to be able to write
/etc/passwd at all; the check above is made against the
.I real
user id.
.PP
.B Note:
the file is rewritten by copying it through /etc/ptmp and copying that
back over /etc/passwd, there being no
.IR rename (2)
on this system.
/etc/passwd keeps its i-number, so nothing holding it open sees it
vanish \- but a crash between the two copies leaves it short.
.PP
.B Note:
/etc/ptmp is also the lock, and it leaks.
Its existence is what makes a second
.I passwd
say `Temporary file busy', and the error paths after it is created do
not remove it \- so an interrupted run leaves every later one refusing.
Remove it by hand.
This is v7's behaviour and is left alone deliberately: the alternative
is unlinking a file this process may not have created.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 0 when the password was changed and 1 otherwise.
.PP
.B Note:
v7 exited 1 in both cases, so no script could tell the two apart.
.SH FILES
/etc/passwd
.br
/etc/ptmp
.br
/etc/utmp
.SH "SEE ALSO"
login(1), su(1), newgrp(1), passwd(5), crypt(3), getlogin(3)
.br
Robert Morris and Ken Thompson,
.I Password Security: A Case History
